docs/DECISIONS.md:31:| D23 | Context snapshots store stable UUIDs (exercise, machine, model, gym), the exercise's **loadType**, and the **freeWeightTag**, alongside display strings; historical queries (prefill, layers, records) group by snapshot values, never live relationships | A "future-only" model correction or a catalog loadType edit must not reinterpret old sets (findings 3; pass-2 criticals 2–3). |
docs/DECISIONS.md:35:| D27 | **Refines D24**: a user-created exercise may be linked to a *seeded* equipment model, and reconciliation **merges** rather than overwrites — a seeded model's `exerciseIDs` becomes the catalog's links (in catalog order) plus the user-added links to `isSeeded == false` exercises the store already held. Stale links to seeded exercises the catalog dropped are still removed. | Ticket 19 lets an unlisted movement be invented at the station it is performed on, and that station is usually a catalog model. `exerciseIDs` is an allowlisted seeded field, so a plain overwrite would silently unlink the user's exercise on the next catalog bump — D24 says reconciliation never touches user-created rows, and dropping a user's link is touching it. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:42:| D34 | **A scan reads the photo and discards it.** No image is written to the store or to disk; only the recognised text (transient) and the model the user picks survive. | Keeping images would mean a schema change, storage that grows with every machine, a decision about whether exports carry pixels, and a CloudKit asset story — all to solve a problem the user did not have. Recognition is on-device (Vision), so no photograph of the user's gym leaves the phone. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:52:| T1 | SwiftData | First-party, SwiftUI-native, fits no-dependency rule. Weak migrations mitigated by simple models + denormalized snapshots. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:53:| T2 | CloudKit-compatible schema; ship single-device | UUIDs/optional relationships/no unique constraints cost little now; retrofitting sync later is a painful migration. |
docs/DECISIONS.md:64:- Import / restore *from* our own JSON — the format is written to be re-importable, but reading it back is post-milestone-3
docs/DECISIONS.md:83:- 2026-08-11 — Codex cross-review of milestone 3 (export): `.scratch/milestone-3-export/codex-review.md`. Verdict "do not merge yet"; three high findings, all real and all fixed on the branch: a **draft entry exported no equipment context** (its D23 snapshot does not exist yet, so the collector now reads live context for uncaptured entries); **D28 could drop a seeded model carrying a D27 user link**; and **`escaped` missed an embedded CRLF** because Swift reads the pair as one Character — the same grapheme trap that had already bitten the test parser. Also fixed: whole-second timestamp truncation (D31), `modelName` renamed `modelDisplayName` (the snapshot stores `manufacturer + model`), per-export temp staging with completion-driven cleanup, and a strict CSV test reader. D30/D31 amended above rather than quietly re-interpreted.
