/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba .scratch/exercise-presets/spec.md | sed -n '25,105p'; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS.md | sed -n '25,60p'; nl -ba .scratch/exercise-presets/issues/01-preset-schema-and-core.md | sed -n '1,55p'; nl -ba .scratch/exercise-presets/issues/03-log-time-picker.md | sed -n '1,40p'; nl -ba .scratch/exercise-presets/issues/04-export-and-verification.md | sed -n '1,45p'" in /Users/ericlee06/orca/projects/Health App
 succeeded in 0ms:
    25	| **Preset** | **Wide grip** | **User, per exercise** |
    26	| Equipment model | Life Fitness Insignia Row | Seeded catalog + user |
    27	| Machine instance | "Cable Row #2" | User, per gym |
    28	| Gym | Gold's Gym Gangnam | User |
    29	
    30	## The three decisions (user, 2026-08-12) → D36–D38
    31	
    32	- **D36 — A preset splits records.** PRs, e1RM, "previous performance" and prefill all key on the
    33	  preset alongside the equipment layers. A narrow-grip row PR is not a wide-grip row PR. **Volume
    34	  is not split**: it is a whole-workout total (D21), and a total that dropped the sets performed
    35	  under another grip would simply be wrong.
    36	  This is the same rule that keeps two machines apart (D1/D8/D23), applied to the axis the user
    37	  actually varies; pooling them would let one variation set a "record" the other can never beat.
    38	- **D37 — Presets belong to the exercise, not the machine.** "Seated Cable Row" offers its grips at
    39	  every gym and on every machine — defined once, travels with you, which is exactly D6's argument
    40	  for gym-independent templates. A machine may nominate *which* of them it usually is.
    41	- **D38 — The preset is chosen at log time, with the machine's usual one preselected.** Grips vary
    42	  session to session on the same machine, so freezing the choice at machine-creation would mean
    43	  editing the machine to change grip. Adding a machine sets its usual preset; logging preselects
    44	  that and switches in one tap.
    45	
    46	## Consequences that follow, not choices
    47	
    48	- **A preset is part of the entry's context snapshot (D23).** It is captured with the rest when the
    49	  entry's first set completes, and history reads the snapshot forever after. Renaming
    50	  "wide grip" later must not retitle last month's sets.
    51	- **Switching preset on a frozen entry starts a new entry (D19).** Identical reasoning to switching
    52	  equipment: completed sets must never be silently relabelled into a variation they were not
    53	  performed in. Uncompleted draft rows move across; completed sets stay.
    54	- **The export carries it (D30).** `presetID`/`presetName` appended as CSV columns 28–29 (appended,
    55	  so a reader of the first 27 is unaffected) and added to every JSON entry; `schemaVersion` → 2.
    56	- **`GymExerciseMemory` is untouched.** It remembers the machine for a (gym, exercise); the
    57	  machine's usual preset lives on the machine.
    58	- **Nothing is seeded.** Presets are user-created (D3's reasoning: a worldwide list of grips per
    59	  machine is unknowable). The add sheet *suggests* common names — it does not ship a catalog.
    60	
    61	## What it looks like
    62	
    63	```
    64	Exercises → Seated Cable Row → Presets
    65	    Wide grip          ⇅
    66	    Narrow grip        ⇅
    67	    Neutral grip       ⇅
    68	    + Add preset…      (suggestions: wide grip, narrow grip, neutral grip,
    69	                        single arm, single leg, high pulley, …)
    70	
    71	Add Machine → Cable Row #2 → Usually: [ Wide grip ▾ ]     (only when the model serves one exercise)
    72	
    73	Active workout → Seated Cable Row
    74	    [ Wide grip ✓ ] [ Narrow grip ] [ Neutral grip ]
    75	    SET  PREVIOUS          WEIGHT  REPS
    76	     1   70 kg × 8 (wide)  [ 70 ]  [ 8 ]  ○
    77	```
    78	
    79	History and the previous-performance sheet name the preset wherever they already name the machine.
    80	
    81	## Out of scope
    82	
    83	- Seeding a preset catalog.
    84	- Presets on free-weight (machineless) entries — the same mechanism will serve them, but the ask
    85	  was about machines; wiring the picker into free-weight entries follows the same entry field.
    86	- Per-preset rest durations, per-preset templates.
    87	- Migrating existing history: sets already logged have no preset and keep none. They group as
    88	  "no preset", which is honest — nobody recorded which grip they were.
    89	
    90	## Tickets
    91	
    92	```
    93	01 preset-schema-and-core   — model, snapshot fields, records/prefill grouping, migration-safe
    94	└── 02 preset-management-ui — presets per exercise, machine's usual preset
    95	    └── 03 log-time-picker  — chips in the entry card, entry split on change, history display
    96	        └── 04 export-and-verification — CSV/JSON, docs, full suite, cross-review
    97	```
    25	| D17 | e1RM: Brzycki, 12-rep record cap | Strong parity (verified from their docs); estimates degrade past 12 reps. |
    26	| D18 | Adopt Strong's template-drift prompt (update/values-only/both/keep) | Elegant, proven solution to template drift. |
    27	| D19 | Equipment context lives on ExerciseEntry and freezes once the entry's first set completes — permanently, even if that completion is later undone. Switching equipment starts a new entry; uncompleted draft rows move to it, completed sets stay | Prevents completed sets being silently relabeled; keeps snapshots, prefill, and machine PRs historically honest (2026-08-08 Codex cross-review, finding 2). |
    28	| D20 | e1RM is computed for `weighted` exercises only. Assisted records = least assistance per rep count; bodyweight+added = most added weight per rep count (athlete bodyweight intentionally ignored); plain bodyweight = most reps, uncapped. The D17 12-rep cap applies only to weight-keyed rep-count tables | Brzycki is mathematically invalid on inverted (assisted) or offset (bodyweight) loads (review finding 5). |
    29	| D21 | Volume = Σ(normalizedKg × reps) over completed working+failure sets of `weighted` exercises. Dumbbells are logged as labeled (per hand) and never auto-doubled. Assisted/bodyweight excluded from volume in v1 | One consistent honest rule; resolves the deferred dumbbell question; avoids Strong's per-platform divergence (finding 14). |
    30	| D22 | Rest-duration precedence: per-exercise override → global defaults (2:00 working / 1:00 warmup). Failure sets use the working duration. TemplateItem carries no rest duration in v1 | Single storage owner; documents the warmup default explicitly (finding 16). |
    31	| D23 | Context snapshots store stable UUIDs (exercise, machine, model, gym), the exercise's **loadType**, and the **freeWeightTag**, alongside display strings; historical queries (prefill, layers, records) group by snapshot values, never live relationships | A "future-only" model correction or a catalog loadType edit must not reinterpret old sets (findings 3; pass-2 criticals 2–3). |
    32	| D24 | Catalog seeding is versioned, idempotent reconciliation keyed by fixed catalog UUIDs. Mutable seeded fields are allowlisted (name, exercise links, metadata); seeded rows are not user-editable — only user-created models/exercises can be renamed | First-launch-only seeding would strand installs; unrestricted edits would fight reconciliation (finding 9; pass-2 important 8). |
    33	| D26 | **Drop sets are a fourth set type** (marker `D`), reopening D12's deferral at the user's request (2026-08-10). Semantics: a drop set is real work, so it counts toward records and volume exactly like a working or failure set — only warmups are excluded. Because a drop set is *by definition* performed without resting, completing one does **not** auto-start the rest timer (D13/D22 unchanged for the other three types). Prefill's type-aware index matching treats drop as its own sequence, consistent with how warmup and working already match. | The user logs drop sets in real training, so the app was silently unable to describe their actual workouts. Counting them toward records is the honest reading — the weight was lifted. Suppressing the timer encodes what makes a drop set a drop set, rather than nagging with a countdown the user will always skip. |
    34	| D25 | Unit conversion uses exactly 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg; `normalizedKg` recomputes atomically on any value/unit edit; storage keeps full precision. Display: up to 2 decimals, trailing zeros trimmed, half-up rounding, locale-independent decimal point in storage; converted values prefixed ≈ | Executable contract so normalized values can never drift from as-entered values (finding 12). |
    35	| D27 | **Refines D24**: a user-created exercise may be linked to a *seeded* equipment model, and reconciliation **merges** rather than overwrites — a seeded model's `exerciseIDs` becomes the catalog's links (in catalog order) plus the user-added links to `isSeeded == false` exercises the store already held. Stale links to seeded exercises the catalog dropped are still removed. | Ticket 19 lets an unlisted movement be invented at the station it is performed on, and that station is usually a catalog model. `exerciseIDs` is an allowlisted seeded field, so a plain overwrite would silently unlink the user's exercise on the next catalog bump — D24 says reconciliation never touches user-created rows, and dropping a user's link is touching it. |
    36	| D28 | **The export carries the user's data, not the shipped catalog.** Seeded exercises and equipment models appear only where the user's data references them (a machine's model, a history snapshot, a template item, a gym memory, a rest override); references are not followed transitively. The file records `seededCatalogVersion` so the omitted rows stay reproducible from the app itself. | A personal backup that is 1877 rows of someone else's catalog and one workout is not a backup of the user's training. The catalog ships in the binary and is versioned (D24); the user's history exists nowhere else. |
    37	| D29 | **Exports carry weights as entered *and* normalized, never converted for display**: `weight`, `unit`, `weightKg`, at full precision. No `≈` value ever reaches a file. | The ≈ marker is a UI honesty device for a *converted* number (D9/D25). A file has room for both exact numbers, so it gets both — and a consumer never has to guess what unit a column is in, which is exactly Strong's failure (SPEC line 88). |
    38	| D30 | **Nothing is silently dropped from an export.** Uncompleted draft sets, entries with no completed sets, and the workout still in progress all export, flagged (`completed`, empty `finishedAt`). Consumers filter as `RecordsMath` does. **Amended 2026-08-11 (codex-review):** the two files carry this differently — JSON is the complete backup; CSV is a complete ledger of *sets*, so a workout or entry holding no set at all has no row in it. A draft entry exports the equipment the user has chosen (live), since its D23 snapshot does not exist until the first set completes. | A backup that omits rows is not a backup; the omission is discovered when the data is needed. Flagging costs one column and keeps the file a faithful copy rather than an interpretation. "One row per set" has nowhere to put a setless object, so the claim is narrowed rather than left false. |
    39	| D31 | **Export timestamps are ISO 8601 with the exporting device's UTC offset and fractional seconds** (`2026-08-11T18:30:00.123+09:00`). **Amended 2026-08-11 (codex-review):** filenames carry local wall time only (`workout-tracker-2026-08-11-1830`) — no offset, no sub-seconds. | A workout log is a record of local time-of-day; bare UTC would file a 6 a.m. Seoul session under the previous evening. Sub-seconds matter because `CanonicalRow` breaks duplicate-row ties on `updatedAt` before the id, so two updates in one second must stay distinct. A filename is for a human sorting files, and an offset in it buys ambiguity, not precision. |
    40	| D32 | **CSV is RFC 4180 with CRLF and a UTF-8 BOM, and user text is written verbatim.** Fields containing quote/comma/CR/LF are quoted with inner quotes doubled; nothing is prefixed to defuse spreadsheet formulas. | The BOM is for Excel, which otherwise mojibakes non-ASCII gym names. Verbatim because the file's whole job is fidelity: rewriting a note that happens to start with `=` corrupts the user's own words to defend them from their own spreadsheet. |
    41	| D33 | **A scanned name plate proposes a catalog model; the user confirms it.** The sheet shows the recognised text and the ranked candidates with scores, and always offers "none of these". Nothing is applied without a tap. A row is **preselected only when all three hold**: score ≥ 0.85, the plate names that row's manufacturer and no other known one, and the runner-up is ≥ 0.08 behind. Amended 2026-08-11 after the cross-review, which found four ways to preselect a wrong row on a score alone. | D23 keys history, prefill and PRs on the model UUID, so a confident wrong match splits the user's own history — the same damage duplicate catalog identities did (codex-review-4). Real plates are scuffed, angled and back-lit, and stablemates share most of their name: a Life Fitness Insignia chest press scores 100% while five siblings sit at 75–79%, so the *distinguishing* word is often the one a camera misses. |
    42	| D34 | **A scan reads the photo and discards it.** No image is written to the store or to disk; only the recognised text (transient) and the model the user picks survive. | Keeping images would mean a schema change, storage that grows with every machine, a decision about whether exports carry pixels, and a CloudKit asset story — all to solve a problem the user did not have. Recognition is on-device (Vision), so no photograph of the user's gym leaves the phone. |
    43	| D35 | **An unmatched scan creates a *user-space* model** (`isSeeded == false`), prefilled with the manufacturer in the catalog's canonical spelling when the brand is known, and the plate's most prominent non-junk line as the model name. Both fields stay editable before saving. | D4: a plausible-but-invented catalog row is worse than an absent one, so OCR output must never masquerade as seeded data. Canonical brand spelling keeps `LIFE FITNESS` from sorting as a different manufacturer than the 200 seeded `Life Fitness` rows, which is a real cost with no upside. |
    44	| D36 | **A preset splits records.** PRs, e1RM, "previous performance" and prefill key on the preset alongside the equipment layers; the preset is part of the entry's D23 snapshot, and switching it on a frozen entry starts a new entry exactly as switching equipment does (D19). Volume, being a whole-workout number, still counts everything. | The user varies grip and single/double on the same machine (2026-08-12). Pooling them lets one variation set a "record" the other can never beat — the same falseness D1/D8 reject across machines, on the axis the user actually trains. A set already logged must never be relabelled into a variation it was not performed in. |
    45	| D37 | **Presets belong to the exercise, not the machine.** "Seated Cable Row" offers its grips at every gym and on every machine; a machine may nominate which one it usually is. User-created only — nothing is seeded, though the add sheet suggests common names. | Same argument as D6's generic templates: a grip is a property of the movement and travels with the user, while retyping it per machine per gym is work with no payoff. A worldwide catalog of every grip on every machine is as unknowable as a worldwide gym database (D3). |
    46	| D38 | **The preset is chosen at log time**, with the machine's usual one preselected and switchable in one tap. A stale machine default (deleted, or belonging to another exercise) degrades to "none chosen". | Grips vary session to session on one machine, so freezing the choice at machine-creation would mean editing the machine to change handles. Preselection removes the tap in the common case without deciding anything the user did not (D33's shape, one feature over). |
    47	
    48	## Technical
    49	
    50	| # | Decision | Why |
    51	|---|---|---|
    52	| T1 | SwiftData | First-party, SwiftUI-native, fits no-dependency rule. Weak migrations mitigated by simple models + denormalized snapshots. |
    53	| T2 | CloudKit-compatible schema; ship single-device | UUIDs/optional relationships/no unique constraints cost little now; retrofitting sync later is a painful migration. |
    54	| T3 | UI-first build order | User requirement: see and approve the screens (sample data) before logic/persistence is wired. |
    55	| T4 | Xcode buildable folders (synchronized groups) | Adding files doesn't touch project.pbxproj — no merge hazard, and agents can add source files without editing project config. |
    56	| T5 | Repo is the source of truth for spec/context | Any agent session (Claude Code or Codex, either dev) loads identical context from docs/. |
    57	| T6 | Cross-review over double-build | Work is reviewed by a **different agent than the one that wrote it** (Claude ↔ Codex), against the tickets and this decision log. Solo development means this is the only independent check that exists — it is not optional ceremony. Proven 2026-08-08: a Codex review of Codex-written code plus a Claude review of it caught 9 real defects, 3 of which broke the product's historical-honesty thesis. Codex may also independently author adversarial tests for the pure-logic core against the spec. |
    58	| T7 | Unit-default precedence: machine → gym → app preference | Most specific context wins. |
    59	
    60	## Deferred / open
